## qf-final | Quick-fire: grab bag | stats | 0 | - | qf-stat
quiz 3 | Which is more likely: flipping 10 heads in a row, or a specific sequence like HTTHTHHTHT?
* They're equally likely
o 10 heads is less likely
o 10 heads is more likely
= Each specific sequence has probability (1/2)¹⁰ = 1/1024.
> Next

quiz 4 | In a room of 30 people, is a shared birthday more likely than not?
* Yes, about 70%
o No, about 30%
o About 8%
o Exactly 50%
= The birthday paradox: pairs add up fast.
> Next

quiz 4 | What does "correlation does not imply causation" warn about?
* Two variables moving together doesn't prove one causes the other
o Statistics are always wrong
o Experiments are useless
o Correlations are always weak
= Confounders and coincidences abound.
> Next

quiz 4 | Survivorship bias means…
* Drawing conclusions only from those who made it through some filter
o Doctors preferring survivors
o Overestimating risks
o Remembering bad events more
= Successful dropouts don't prove dropping out works.
> Next

quiz 4 | A "p-hacking" researcher…
* Tries many analyses until one gives p < 0.05
o Publishes negative results
o Pre-registers their study
o Uses large samples
= It inflates false positives.
> Next

quiz 4 | What does a 95% confidence interval's width mainly depend on?
* Sample size and variability
o The p-value
o The researcher's confidence
o The population size
= Bigger samples and less spread give narrower intervals.
> Grab bag complete
